  "title": "Restarts E2c: the in-place executor runs as a launchd service, and every deploy outcome leaves it on the daemon's release or confirmed off",
  "body": "## Problem\nPart of #784 (slice E2c), under M6.2.\n\n#815 (PR #842) added `limitless executor`: a process that leases in-place gate jobs from the coordinator's database and runs them in the run's worktree. Gates survive a daemon restart only while that process is running. Nothing starts it, keeps it running or keeps it on the right release, so in practice gates still run in-process.\n\n- **No service unit.**\n  - `src/cli/service.ts:35-37` defines three units: daemon, tunnel and mtplx.\n  - `installationUnits` (`service.ts:271-296`) writes only those.\n  - `installedUnits` (`service.ts:177-218`) recognizes only `daemon`, `mtplx` and `tunnel`, and returns each unit's actual `Label` and path, which needn't be the canonical label.\n  - `limitless service install` (`src/cli/main.ts:661-665`) has no executor option.\n  - So the executor is a foreground command (`main.ts:421-432`).\n- **It never moves to a new release.**\n  - It resolves its release once at start (`main.ts:426`).\n  - It leases only while the daemon's recorded release (`executor.daemonRelease`, written at daemon boot in `src/cli/daemon.ts:55`) equals its own (`src/db/workers.ts:425-437`).\n  - After the daemon moves, it cancels its jobs (`src/gates/inplace.ts:38-47`) and then idles forever (`inplace.ts:28-31`). Every later gate falls back to in-process with a warning (`src/gates/lan.ts:63-71`).\n- **Deploy rollback can't guarantee it is stopped.**\n  - The rollback's `cleanup` helper records each failure and carries on (`service.ts:773-784`). A failed shutdown placed in it would still be followed by restoring older code under a live executor.\n  - An interruption after the restart skips rollback entirely (`service.ts:770`).\n  - Deploy keeps no durable journal, unlike installation (`service.ts:330-375`), so a deploy process that dies mid-rollback leaves no record of what was still to do.\n  - An executor left loaded on a rolled-back checkout that lacks the `executor` command is restarted every 10 s by `KeepAlive` (`ThrottleInterval`, `service.ts:116`). launchd also loads every plist in the LaunchAgents directory again at the next login.\n- **A deploy can end successfully in two ways.**\n  - The same-SHA path returns early (`service.ts:727-740`). That's how `limitless deploy \u003csha\u003e` recovers after a rollback.\n  - `--now` only changes the drain (`service.ts:752-766`). It is not a separate path.\n\n## Invariant\n- **After any deploy or `service install` that succeeds (exits 0) with the daemon healthy on release R:**\n  - if `[executor] enabled` is true and the executor unit is installed, the executor is loaded and enabled, and runs R within one launchd restart interval;\n  - if `[executor] enabled` is false, it is confirmed unloaded and disabled.\n- **After any rollback, and after recovering from a deploy that ended without one of the outcomes above** (an interruption, or process death): the executor is confirmed unloaded and disabled before code from a different release is checked out. If that can't be confirmed, no checkout change happens.\n- **`limitless restart` (same release)** never stops or restarts the executor. Its running jobs carry on and are re-attached (#815).\n\n## Requirements\nThis is one deliverable. Don't ship the unit without the rollback shutdown and the reconciliation, because a loaded unit without them is exactly the unsafe state above.\n\n1. **The unit.**\n   - `installationUnits` adds `dev.limitless.executor` when the config's `executorEnabled` is true:\n     - source: `[\u003cbun\u003e, \u003capp\u003e/src/cli/main.ts, \"executor\"]`;\n     - packaged: `[stableExecutable(), \"executor\"]`.\n   - It uses the daemon's `plist()`: the same `PATH` and `LANG`, `KeepAlive`, and working directory.\n   - `installedUnits` recognizes it as kind `executor` for both install kinds, under the existing `Program` rule (#809) and the `LimitlessService` marker rule. It returns the actual label and path.\n   - Every step below that acts on the executor first resolves it through `installedUnits`. A fixed label is never assumed.\n2. **One executor-control helper** in `service.ts`, used by install, deploy and recovery.\n   - `shutdownExecutor(unit)`:\n     - `launchctl bootout gui/\u003cuid\u003e/\u003clabel\u003e`;\n     - poll `launchctl print` until it reports not loaded, with the bound and interval of install's `stop` (`service.ts:319-325`);\n     - `launchctl disable gui/\u003cuid\u003e/\u003clabel\u003e`.\n     - It returns only when both the unload and the disable are confirmed, and otherwise throws.\n   - `reconcileExecutor(enabled)`:\n     - with `enabled`, and an executor unit installed: `launchctl enable`, then `bootstrap` if not loaded;\n     - when `!enabled`: `shutdownExecutor`.\n     - It never writes or deletes a plist; that's `service install`'s job.\n3. **A durable intent.** The file `\u003cdeploy lock dir\u003e/executor-intent.json`, written with the journal's atomic write (`service.ts:332-335`).\n   - **Contents:** `{ phase: \"deploying\" | \"rollback\", label, path, previous, target }`.\n   - **When:** written before a deploy changes the checkout, and rewritten as `rollback` before the rollback's shutdown. It is deleted only after `reconcileExecutor` has succeeded at the end of a successful deploy or `service install`.\n   - **Recovery:** the start of every `deploy` and `service install` already runs installation recovery (`install(…, { recover: true })`, `service.ts:709`). Extend that step:\n     - an intent file present means the previous deploy didn't finish, so run `shutdownExecutor` on its recorded unit;\n     - if that can't be confirmed, refuse to continue, with recovery instructions: the exact `launchctl bootout` and `launchctl disable` commands for the recorded label, then rerun the command.\n4. **Deploy** (`deploy()`, `service.ts:674-800`).\n   - **Rollback,** whenever a deploy rolls back (gate failure or failed restart, with or without `--now`):\n     - `shutdownExecutor` runs first, before `restore previous release`, outside the error-collecting `cleanup` helper;\n     - if it throws, the rollback stops before the checkout is restored, and the error names the recorded unit, the target and previous SHAs, and the recovery commands. The intent file stays.\n   - **Success:** every deploy that succeeds runs `reconcileExecutor(executorEnabled)` before returning, then deletes the intent. That includes:\n     - a new release (after `waitForHealthy`);\n     - the same-SHA path, including after a rollback;\n     - `--now`.\n   - **Interruption after the restart** (`service.ts:770`): rollback is still skipped, as today, and the intent file stays. The next `deploy` or `service install` shuts the executor down first (requirement 3). Then, if it ends healthy, it reconciles.\n5. **`service install`.**\n   - It reconciles with the config after the daemon unit is healthy:\n     - enabled: install or replace the executor plist through the existing journaled loop (`service.ts:394-446`), then `reconcileExecutor(true)` (`enable` before `bootstrap`);\n     - disabled: `shutdownExecutor`, then remove its plist.\n   - **Restoring after a failed replacement** goes through the install journal's `restore` (`service.ts:339-350`), which today re-bootstraps the old unit. For the executor it restores the old plist but leaves it unloaded and disabled. A failed install never leaves an executor running on an unconfirmed release; the next successful deploy or install reconciles it.\n   - **Recovery must not loop on that restoration.** Today recovery skips a `prepared` or `restored` journal only while the old unit is loaded. Otherwise it restores again and throws \"retry installation\" (`service.ts:352-375`), so an executor deliberately left unloaded would stop every later deploy and install before reconciliation.\n     - An executor journal in phase `restored`, whose unit `shutdownExecutor` confirms is unloaded and disabled, counts as completed restoration: recovery neither restores it again nor throws, and installation or deployment proceeds.\n     - That journal is deleted only after a later successful `reconcileExecutor`.\n     - If the unload can't be confirmed, recovery refuses, with requirement 3's instructions.\n     - Daemon, mtplx and tunnel journals keep today's rule.\n6. **The executor follows the release** (`runInplaceExecutor`, `src/gates/inplace.ts`).\n   - It is *serving* once it has read `executor.daemonRelease` equal to its own release at least once, whether or not it has leased anything.\n   - Once serving, if the setting differs from its release: it stops its current job (the existing `checkLease` cancel), then returns. launchd restarts it from the release checkout.\n   - Until it is serving, a mismatch only idles, as today. This covers a deploy that has switched the checkout but hasn't yet restarted the daemon.\n   - Idle polling stays read-only (#815's rule).\n7. **Restart.** `restart()` (`service.ts:577-623`) makes no executor launchctl calls.\n8. **Docs.**\n   - GUIDE `[executor] enabled`: the unit, the release-following rule, and that a rollback leaves the executor disabled until the next healthy deploy or install.\n   - OPERATIONS: the intent file, the recovery commands, and `limitless service status`.\n\n## Tests\nEach must fail without its change. Use test/service.test.ts's real temporary plists and the fake `plutil`/`launchctl` runner (never real `launchctl`), extending the fake to model `enable`/`disable` and to refuse `bootstrap` of a disabled label, as launchd does. Use real subprocesses and a temporary database for the executor, as test/gate-inplace-executor.test.ts does.\n\n**Unit and recognition**\n1. With the executor enabled, `installationUnits` emits the source and the packaged arguments above, `KeepAlive` and the daemon's environment. With it disabled, it emits none.\n2. Recognition:\n   - an executor plist of either kind is recognized, including one under a non-canonical label;\n   - a plist with a different `Program`, or with a `LimitlessService` of `daemon`, is not;\n   - two executor plists raise \"multiple installed\".\n\n**Shutdown, rollback and recovery (adversarial)**\n\n3. **Failed bootout.** On a rollback, `bootout` fails, or `print` keeps reporting loaded. The checkout isn't restored (no `git checkout` in the fake runner's log after the failure), the error names the recovery commands, and the intent file remains.\n4. **Order.** On a rollback, the executor is confirmed unloaded and disabled before the previous commit is checked out and before the previous daemon restarts, for both a failed gate and a failed restart, and with `--now`. A recognized executor under a non-canonical label is the one shut down.\n5. **Interrupted cleanup.** The deploy process is killed after writing the `rollback` intent and before the shutdown is confirmed, simulated by throwing from the fake. The next `limitless deploy`:\n   - completes the shutdown before touching the checkout;\n   - refuses, with instructions, if the shutdown still can't be confirmed;\n   - otherwise proceeds, and on success re-enables the executor.\n6. **Interrupted after restart.** The process is interrupted after the restart is attempted. The intent stays at `deploying`. The next `service install` shuts the executor down first, then reconciles it on once healthy.\n\n**Reconciliation**\n\n7. A successful deploy, the same-SHA path after a rollback, and a `--now` deploy each end with the executor enabled and loaded when the config enables it, and confirmed off when it doesn't. The intent file is gone in every case.\n8. `service install` after a rollback-disable succeeds: `enable` precedes `bootstrap`.\n9. A failing executor replacement during `service install` restores the old plist, which stays unloaded and disabled. A previously disabled executor isn't re-enabled by that restoration.\n10. **Two consecutive recoveries.** After such a restoration:\n    - a first `limitless deploy` (or `service install`) runs recovery, finds the `restored` executor journal with the unit confirmed unloaded and disabled, and proceeds without \"retry installation\";\n    - a second invocation does the same;\n    - the journal is gone only after a successful reconciliation;\n    - with the unload unconfirmed, both invocations refuse with the instructions.\n\n**Executor process (real subprocess)**\n\n11. An executor observes release A and leases nothing; the setting then changes to B. It exits with code 0 within two ticks. No job is required for it to count as serving.\n12. An executor holding a job when the setting changes fails the job (not left leased), sets `stopped`, and exits.\n13. An executor started at B while the setting says A neither exits nor writes, until the setting becomes B. Then it leases.\n\n**Restart**\n\n14. With the unit loaded and an in-place job running, `limitless restart` makes no launchctl call naming the executor. The restarted daemon re-attaches to the same job through its durable handle, and the check's command runs exactly once (extending #815's restart test).\n\n## Compatibility\n- **Rollback to a release without the executor:** requirement 4 confirms the unit is unloaded and disabled before the older code is checked out, and the disable survives the next login.\n  - An older `service install` or `status` ignores the plist: its `installedUnits` matches only `… serve`.\n  - An older CLI ignores the intent file.\n- **Rollback to a release that has #842 but not this change:** the executor stays disabled, and gates run in-process with the existing warning, as today.\n- **Cost of a failed deploy:** any rolled-back deploy, including a failed deploy gate where the daemon never moved, leaves the executor disabled until the next successful deploy or install. Gates run in-process meanwhile. That is today's behaviour, and it is chosen over guessing which release a half-finished deploy left running.\n- **Install journal:** executor entries use the existing `Migration` shape. An older CLI recovering one would re-bootstrap it, and that fails while the unit is disabled; the failure is reported, not hidden. OPERATIONS says so.\n- **Restart and resume:** `limitless restart` and a daemon crash leave the executor and its jobs alone, and re-attach follows #815. A launchd restart of the executor itself fails its leased jobs, as a crash does today. `waitStopped` and process recovery cover them (#815, #817).\n- **Polling only:** no webhook involvement.\n- **Config:** no new key. `[executor] enabled` exists, and an older release ignores it.\n- **Packaged installs:** `brew upgrade` plus `service install` replaces and reconciles the unit. Packaged deploys stay refused (#38 slice 7).\n\n## Out of scope\n- Land checks through the executor (E2b).\n- Agent sessions in the executor (E3).\n- Deploy without a drain, and blue/green handover (E4).\n- Evals in the executor (E5).\n- Linux coordinators: no launchd.\n- Packaged-mode deploy (#38 slice 7).\n- Rollback of the daemon unit itself: unchanged.\n\n## Budget\nAbout 190 changed source lines plus tests:\n- unit and recognition ~30;\n- the control helper with confirmation ~35;\n- intent file and recovery ~35;\n- deploy rollback and success wiring ~35;\n- install reconciliation, restore state and the recovery rule for a restored executor ~35;\n- executor release-following ~12;\n- CLI config wiring ~8.\n\nIt is indivisible: if it passes 250, stop and report instead of shipping part of it.\n"
